From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: utzcoz <utzcoz@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 20:59:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] util: implement the sys layer for Darwin

The sys layer resolved to the stub backend on Apple, so every tube, event and
wait context failed at runtime.

Tubes and events are Mach ports. mach_msg is message oriented, which Darwin's
sockets are not, and a port descriptor can carry an IOSurface, which they
cannot. A port right cannot cross a socket, so the two ends still meet on one
and use it to agree on a name for a single introduction through
bootstrap_check_in. The name is random per connection and is retired with the
right it came with.

A receive right and a send right are separate types, since one lets an end wait
and the other only signal, and a port takes a queue limit of 128 messages.
Readiness is kqueue: EVFILT_READ for the listener, EVFILT_MACHPORT for a tube.
EV_EOF means nothing for a port, so a peer hanging up is asked of the kernel
instead. A raw descriptor is an i64, because a Mach port name does not fit an
int.

Building and reading a message is a module of its own, which tubes and events
share. An outgoing message owns the rights it is going to carry until the
kernel takes them, so one that is never sent releases them instead of leaving
this task holding names it has given away.

The modules sit under sys/darwin and are selected with target_vendor, so iOS
takes the same backend.

Reviewed-by: Gurchetan Singh  <gurchetan.singh.foss@gmail.com>
